{"kind":"assert","text":"Disposition of item 1 (ea7e2aa6): adopted. The rule is in force from this assert.\n\nA design note, plan or request that adds any of these waits while K1 to K11 are open, unless it names the K item that it closes:\n(a) a scope kind;\n(b) a platform definition name;\n(c) a capability name or form;\n(d) a tool family: a new group of MCP tools or of CLI commands;\n(e) a new model of the record: a new entry kind, a new level, a new field type, or a new kind of reference between scopes.\nA head closes a K item when it does what docs/kernel.md section 5 says passing takes for that item. The checker judges that at the head. A filing that adds a new part that fails the kernel test waits in every case.\n\nNot held:\n- work that only uses existing forms: applications, packages, views, documents, tests and fixes;\n- a new version of an existing platform definition; it is not a new name, but it says that it adds one to K1's count;\n- fixes for the filed security findings (R1 to R16, D1 to D6); they still follow docs/kernel.md section 7 and add no K item.\n\nCommitted K filings: K7 and K8 (in 7b19837c), K9, and K2 with K3 and K11 close K items, so the rule does not hold them. K9 and K2+K3+K11 file by 23 October, 17:00.\n\nWork in flight:\n- Counter slices 2 to 6, after RI2: each goes ahead if it adds none of (a) to (e). A slice that adds one waits or names a K item.\n- History slice H1: documents only; goes ahead.\n- Layout note (1fcd6692): goes ahead and takes K10 into its scope; its slices close K10.\n- Guard grammar (e949b0df): goes ahead; its slices close K5.\n- OAuth (3f598e55): goes ahead as a service at the hosted endpoint if it adds no record form. If it needs one, it waits or names a K item.\n- Room fork: waits. It is a new model of the record. After the K1 design (50133470) lands, the planner checks whether it can use the definition succession forms instead.\n- Builder requests filed before this rule (retained files RF1 to RF4, capture CS1 to CS4, RI1 to RI4): when each reaches the front of the queue, its head states its kernel placement under docs/kernel.md section 7. A head that adds to the kernel names the K item that it closes, or the planner holds it.\n\ndocs/kernel.md section 7 records this rule in the document head of request ba39a946."}